hostap-s1g (2.1.0-2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * debian/rules: enable 802.11s mesh support (CONFIG_MESH) in
    wpa_supplicant_s1g.
  * debian/rules: enable the PEAP, MD5, MSCHAPv2, TLS (including TLS 1.3),
    TTLS, GTC and PWD EAP methods in wpa_supplicant_s1g so it can join
    WPA-Enterprise (802.1X) networks.
  * debian/rules: stop appending CONFIG_IEEE80211AH, CONFIG_S1G_TWT,
    CONFIG_MORSE_STANDBY_MODE and CONFIG_MORSE_KEEP_ALIVE_OFFLOAD to the
    wpa_supplicant configuration; the upstream defconfig already enables
    them.
  * Drop the D-Bus integration from wpa-supplicant-s1g; it never worked.
    wpa_supplicant_s1g is built from the upstream defconfig, which leaves
    CONFIG_CTRL_IFACE_DBUS_NEW commented out, so the binary has no D-Bus
    code at all and rejects the '-u' flag. The package nevertheless shipped
    a bus policy and an activation file for fi.w1.wpa_supplicant1_s1g, a
    name that appears nowhere in the upstream sources, so the bus advertised
    a service that could never be acquired and every client attempting to
    reach it blocked for the full 25s activation timeout.
    - debian/wpa-supplicant-s1g.dbus-system-policy.conf,
      debian/wpa-supplicant-s1g.dbus-system-service: removed.
    - debian/rules: drop the corresponding install steps.
    - debian/control: drop the now-unused libdbus-1-dev build dependency.
    Enabling D-Bus instead was considered and rejected: both NetworkManager
    and WPAS_DBUS_NEW_SERVICE hardcode fi.w1.wpa_supplicant1, so the only
    working configuration would contend with Debian's wpasupplicant for that
    name. See debian/README.md.
  * debian/wpa-supplicant-s1g.service: replace the D-Bus activation unit
    with a plain daemon exposing a global control socket at
    /run/wpa-supplicant-s1g/global (Type=simple, '-g'). The previous unit
    was Type=dbus with BusName=fi.w1.wpa_supplicant1_s1g and ran the
    supplicant with '-u', which merely printed the usage message and exited,
    leaving an enabled unit that did nothing on every boot.
  * debian/rules: do not enable or start wpa-supplicant-s1g.service on
    install. Now that the unit runs a real daemon it would otherwise sit
    idle with no interfaces attached; the per-interface template unit
    wpa-supplicant-s1g@<iface>.service remains the normal way to bring up a
    link. Hosts that had the old unit enabled will need to disable it if
    they do not want the daemon.
  * debian/README.md: document that NetworkManager cannot manage a HaLow
    interface, why, and what to do instead.
  * debian/hostapd-s1g.service: skip the unit unless
    /etc/hostapd-s1g/hostapd.conf exists and is non-empty
    (ConditionFileNotEmpty). The unit is enabled on install but the package
    ships no AP configuration, so until an admin writes one it failed on
    every boot; it is now skipped cleanly instead, and still starts by
    itself once the configuration is in place. Matches the condition
    Debian's own hostapd package uses.

 -- Austin <vidplace7@gmail.com>  Tue, 22 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

hostap-s1g (2.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New upstream release (upstream tag mm8108-2.1.0).
  * debian/watch: track the per-chip 'mm8108-' tags; upstream now tags the
    MM6108 and MM8108 series separately, so the stale note about upstream
    having no tags is dropped.
  * debian/watch: match the archive extension in filenamemangle so the
    downloaded tarball is not named hostap-s1g-<version>.tar.gz.tar.gz.
  * debian/rules: fetch the 'mm8108-<upstream version>' tag in
    get-orig-source, derived from the changelog version.
  * debian/copyright: extend Morse Micro copyright years to 2026.

